OS Scheduling & Context Switching  

CPU & Register Organization I note that the 16‑bit Program Counter, known as PC tracks the address of the instruction to execute. The Accumulator, called ACC 
and the B Register manage the arithmetic and logic operations. The Program Status Word, abbreviated PSW, stores flags and decides which register bank is active.
CPU employs eight working registers R0 through R7 to hold data while running. 

Memory Layout The chip contains 256 bytes of RAM. The lower 128 bytes hold the register banks and the system stack. The Special Function Registers, abbreviated 
SFRs are mapped to memory addresses 0x80 through 0xFF to control hardware peripherals. The microcontroller provides 8 Kilobytes of Flash ROM for storing program 
instructions. 

Timers & Interrupt Mechanism the STC89C52 includes three 16‑bit timers: Timer 0 Timer 1 and Timer 2. For the OS simulator, Timer 0 or Timer 2 will act as the OS
Tick generating a hardware at fixed interrupt intervals to trigger the process scheduler. When an interrupt occurs hardware automatically stops the task pushes
PC onto the stack and redirects execution to the interrupt vector address. 

Peripherals & Instructions Four 8‑bit GPIO ports P0, P1, P2 and P3 manage digital input and output signals. The simulators execution engine will need to decode
and process instruction categories, including data transfers, arithmetic, logic operations and branching. 
